# Secrets & Configuration

- Never hardcode secrets, API keys, tokens, passwords, or connection strings in source code,
  commit messages, or documentation.
- All configuration must be sourced from environment variables in local/dev and from Azure Key
  Vault (via managed identity) in deployed environments — never from a committed file.
- `.env` files are never committed (enforced via `.gitignore`). Only `.env.example` is
  committed, and it must contain placeholder values only, never real secrets.
- When a new configuration value is introduced, add a documented placeholder entry to
  `.env.example` in the same change.
- If a task appears to require a real secret to proceed (e.g. a live API key), stop and ask the
  user to provide it via a secure mechanism (e.g. Cursor Dashboard secrets) rather than
  inventing or hardcoding one.
